%S A215808 3,3,17,41,47,67,151,167,199,227,251,257,347,367,557,587,601,607,641,
%T A215808 647,727,941,971,1051,1091,1097,1117,1181,1217,1277,1361,1427,1447,
%U A215808 1447,1487,1487,1499,1607,1697,1741,1747,1741,1777,1877,1901,2087,2143,2281
%N A215808 Primes of the form 2*prime(k) - prime(k+1).
%C A215808 Corresponding values of k: 3, 4, 9, 15, 16, 21, 37, 40, 47, 51, 55, 56, 71, 74, 103 (A216075).
%C A215808 Geometric interpretation: Let prime(n) denote the prime with index n. For each n, place a circle centered at x = prime(n) on the x-axis, with radius prime(n+1) - prime(n), the gap to the next prime. Its right x-intercept is prime(n+1), while its left x-intercept is 2*prime(n) - prime(n+1). Therefore a term of the sequence is a prime x-coordinate at which the right intercept of one prime-gap circle centered at prime(i) coincides with the left intercept of a later circle centered at prime(j). If several later circles have the same left intercept, the corresponding prime occurs with the corresponding multiplicity. Equivalently, the terms are the primes satisfying prime(i+1) = 2*prime(j) - prime(j+1), where i and j are positive integers with j >= i+2, with one occurrence for each admissible pair (i,j). - _Rui Ferreira_, Sep 16 2026
%H A215808 Zak Seidov, <a href="/A215808/b215808.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..1000</a>
%H A215808 Rui Ferreira, <a href="/A215808/a215808.jpg">Graphic representation of a(n) for n = 1..6</a>
%e A215808 k=3: 2*5-7=3, k=4: 2*7-11=3, k=9: 2*23-29=17.
%t A215808 pr=Prime[Range[1000]]; s=Select[2*Most[pr]-Rest[pr],PrimeQ]
%t A215808 (* Alternative: *)
%t A215808 Select[2#[[1]]-#[[2]]&/@Partition[Prime[Range[500]],2,1],PrimeQ] (* _Harvey P. Dale_, Feb 25 2017 *)
%Y A215808 Cf. A062234, A085704, A094104, A094105, A216075.
%K A215808 nonn,changed
